{"id":"tyramine","name":"Tyramine","form":"capsule","status":"Reference","source":"Wikidata","wikidata":"Q165930","aliases":"4-(2-aminoethyl)phenol, 2-(4-Hydroxyphenyl)ethylamine, 2-(4'-Hydroxyphenyl)ethylamine, 4- (2-Aminoethyl)-Phenol, 4-(2-Aminoethyl)-Phenol, 4-(2-Aminoethyl)-phenol(thyramin), 4-Hydroxy-b-phenylethylamine, 4-Hydroxy-Benzeneethanamine","category":"Psychedelic","chemicalClass":"Substituted phenethylamines","groups":[],"atc":[],"summary":"Tyramine ( TY-rə-meen) (also spelled tyramin), also known under several other names, is a naturally occurring trace amine derived from the amino acid tyrosine. Tyramine acts as a catecholamine releasing agent. Notably, it is unable to cross the blood-brain barrier, resulting in only non-psychoactive peripheral sympathomimetic effects following ingestion.","summarySources":"Wikipedia","doseUnit":"","doseRows":[],"durationRows":[],"subjectiveEffects":[],"combinations":[],"citations":[],"helpfulLinks":[{"title":"Wikipedia","description":"Encyclopedia article","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyramine"},{"title":"Wikidata","description":"Structured data on Tyramine","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q165930"},{"title":"PubChem","description":"Chemistry, safety and literature","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/5610"}]}